[{"short_id":"nwdxko","created_at":"2026-09-30T17:06:21.893-05:00","title":"EDG C/C++ Compiler Project","url":"https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler","score":1,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"\u003cp\u003eDuring the 2025 November C++ ISO meeting Edison Design Group \u003ca href=\"https://herbsutter.com/2025/11/10/trip-report-november-2025-iso-c-standards-meeting-kona-usa/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003eannounced that they would be winding down and that they will open-source their front end\u003c/a\u003e. This is the repo containing said front end along with some other components like C and C++ back ends. There is also a \u003ca href=\"https://edgcpp.org/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ewebsite\u003c/a\u003e with more info about the transition and a \u003ca href=\"https://edgcpp.org/doc/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003edocumentation website\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAt least as far as my knowledge extends this is one of the four still extant C++ front ends (Clang, GCC, and MSVC being the others). It's most notably used as the front end for Visual Studio's Intellisense, and Intel's compiler used to use it before they switched to Clang.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnother interesting historical tidbit is that they were the only front end to implement C++98's \u003ccode\u003eexport template\u003c/code\u003e feature, and their experience was  \u003ca href=\"https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2003/n1426.pdf\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003eused to argue for the removal of the feature in C++11\u003c/a\u003e, though according to \u003ca href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/5416872/using-export-keyword-with-templates#comment6130296_5416981\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ethis Stack Overflow comment\u003c/a\u003e that specific paper didn't pass voting. The feature was eventually removed in C++11, but I'm unable to find exactly when and/or which paper did so. There's a \u003ca href=\"https://herbsutter.com/2009/10/23/deprecating-export-considered-for-iso-c0x/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003e2009 blog post by Herb Sutter\u003c/a\u003e indicating support for removal/deprecation with deprecation getting the most support, so I guess the paper/vote happened sometime after that.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"During the 2025 November C++ ISO meeting Edison Design Group [announced that they would be winding down and that they will open-source their front end](https://herbsutter.com/2025/11/10/trip-report-november-2025-iso-c-standards-meeting-kona-usa/). This is the repo containing said front end along with some other components like C and C++ back ends. There is also a [website](https://edgcpp.org/) with more info about the transition and a [documentation website](https://edgcpp.org/doc/).\r\n\r\nAt least as far as my knowledge extends this is one of the four still extant C++ front ends (Clang, GCC, and MSVC being the others). It's most notably used as the front end for Visual Studio's Intellisense, and Intel's compiler used to use it before they switched to Clang.\r\n\r\nAnother interesting historical tidbit is that they were the only front end to implement C++98's `export template` feature, and their experience was  [used to argue for the removal of the feature in C++11](https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2003/n1426.pdf), though according to [this Stack Overflow comment](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/5416872/using-export-keyword-with-templates#comment6130296_5416981) that specific paper didn't pass voting. The feature was eventually removed in C++11, but I'm unable to find exactly when and/or which paper did so. There's a [2009 blog post by Herb Sutter](https://herbsutter.com/2009/10/23/deprecating-export-considered-for-iso-c0x/) indicating support for removal/deprecation with deprecation getting the most support, so I guess the paper/vote happened sometime after that.","submitter_user":"aw1621107","user_is_author":false,"tags":["c","c++","compilers"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/nwdxko","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/nwdxko/edg_c_c_compiler_project"},{"short_id":"0xfpgo","created_at":"2026-09-30T15:28:27.900-05:00","title":"We used a database as a message queue. Now we use Kafka","url":"https://www.tigrisdata.com/blog/quick-fdb-kafka/","score":4,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"billy","user_is_author":false,"tags":["databases","distributed"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/0xfpgo","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/0xfpgo/we_used_database_as_message_queue_now_we"},{"short_id":"2r2sg8","created_at":"2026-09-30T14:57:54.701-05:00","title":"Finding Bugs","url":"https://matklad.github.io/2026/09/19/finding-bugs.html","score":4,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"hwayne","user_is_author":false,"tags":["rust","testing"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/2r2sg8","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/2r2sg8/finding_bugs"},{"short_id":"t00ipa","created_at":"2026-09-30T13:52:01.525-05:00","title":"Software occlusion culling in Block Game","url":"https://enikofox.com/posts/software-rendered-occlusion-culling-in-block-game/","score":1,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"FedericoSchonborn","user_is_author":false,"tags":["games","graphics"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/t00ipa","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/t00ipa/software_occlusion_culling_block_game"},{"short_id":"y3ppje","created_at":"2026-09-30T13:06:19.835-05:00","title":"Branch Target Reuse: Spectre-v2 Attacks in JIT Engines","url":"https://www.vusec.net/projects/btr/","score":6,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"fro","user_is_author":false,"tags":["security"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/y3ppje","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/y3ppje/branch_target_reuse_spectre_v2_attacks"},{"short_id":"3ixiwz","created_at":"2026-09-30T12:10:52.107-05:00","title":"Qt 6.12 LTS Released","url":"https://www.qt.io/blog/qt-6.12-released","sc